        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Myths · MYTH·08</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Mate for Life?</h1>
    <div class="id-verdict id-verdict--partly">
      <span class="id-verdict-label">Verdict: Partly True</span>
      <p>Ravens form long-term pair bonds, often lasting many years. But divorce occurs, and widowed birds re-pair. "Mate for life" is a simplification.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens typically form <strong>long-term pair bonds</strong> that endure for many years — often until a partner dies. The bond is maintained year-round through territorial defence and mutual activity, not just during breeding. However, divorce has been documented (voluntary dissolution while both partners survive), and widowed birds re-pair. "Mate for life" captures the general pattern but overstates the permanence.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>What "Mating for Life" Actually Means in Birds</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ornithologists use precise language to avoid the ambiguity of popular phrases:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Social monogamy:</strong> A male and female maintain a social pair bond and co-raise offspring — does not imply genetic fidelity</li>
      <li><strong>Pair bond duration:</strong> How long a social pair persists — session-to-session, year-to-year, or longer</li>
      <li><strong>Divorce rate:</strong> The rate at which both members of a pair that has previously bred together fail to breed together again (while both are alive)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Ravens are socially monogamous with long pair bonds. The question is about pair bond duration and divorce rate, not genetic fidelity (which is harder to measure).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>What the Evidence Shows</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Long-term bonds are typical:</strong> Pairs observed across multiple breeding seasons in the same territory are common in long-term study populations (UK, Maine, Yellowstone area)</li>
      <li><strong>Year-round bond maintenance:</strong> Raven pairs defend territories together throughout the year — the bond is not just seasonal</li>
      <li><strong>Divorce occurs:</strong> Boarman & Heinrich (1999) note that voluntary pair dissolution (both birds surviving, different partners the following year) has been documented, though the rate is not precisely quantified for ravens in most studies</li>
      <li><strong>Widowhood and re-pairing:</strong> When one partner dies, the surviving bird typically re-pairs with a floater bird from the non-territorial population — often fairly quickly, as territories are valuable and unpaired territory-holding birds attract competitors</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The honest summary: ravens show long-term social monogamy with low divorce rates — consistent with popular descriptions of "mating for life" as a generalisation, while not being unconditionally permanent.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Why Long Pair Bonds Evolved</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Long pair bonds are advantageous for ravens for several reasons:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Territory maintenance:</strong> Ravens hold territories year-round; a stable pair defends the territory more effectively than two birds that must re-negotiate annually</li>
      <li><strong>Breeding experience:</strong> Established pairs that have bred together previously show higher breeding success than new pairs — they have learned each other's foraging ranges and coordination patterns</li>
      <li><strong>Investment payoff:</strong> Raven chicks require extensive parental investment; a stable pair partnership amortises the cost of that investment over multiple breeding seasons</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These are functional explanations, not evidence for emotion or "love" in the human sense — though raven pair bonding does involve extensive mutual grooming, food sharing, and coordinated behaviour that reflect strong social bonds.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="id-faq-item">
        <summary class="id-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="id-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
